// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5.6 statistics
B.push(H2('5.6. Statistics and what the uncertainty covers', 'stats'));
B.push(P(`Proportions carry Clopper–Pearson 95% intervals (Clopper & Pearson, 1934). Paired comparisons of in-window rates use the exact McNemar test (McNemar, 1947). Holm's correction (Holm, 1979) is applied over the seven competitors at each level of the identical-protocol comparison, over the nine ablation variants, and, for the health-indicator alarm, over the seven datasets of the timing-control table (six engine datasets for its calibrated version). The constant-estimate comparisons are reported unadjusted, since none was significant even before adjustment. The pre-specified hypothesis tests are reported unadjusted, as fixed in the protocols. A non-significant difference is not read as equivalence, because no equivalence margin was specified. Ratios of cost rates carry paired 95% intervals from 2,000 bootstrap resamples of test units with the fitted policies held fixed. The 16 lead-time and cost-ratio cells of a dataset share the same units and alarms, so they are not independent replications; we report the range of ratios over cells and, descriptively, the number of cells whose interval excludes 1.`));
B.push(P(`These intervals cover the sampling of test units given the fitted policies. They omit the variability from refitting the regressor, from the assignment of units to folds and from the selection of settings. Three results indicate its size. On PHM08, thirty repeated random 50-engine test sets with settings re-selected each time gave a standard deviation of ${f1(N.rep30.acc_sd)} percentage points (Supplementary Section S9). On the batteries, changing the time scale (κ = 2 to 6) moved the cross-validation rate by up to ${f1(BPkapRange.cv)} points and the held-out-batch rate by up to ${f1(BPkapRange.lobo)} points. A change of the filling of one missing cycle moved per-split counts by up to three cells (Supplementary Section S5). Four leakage checks were run on PHM08: disjoint outer and inner units, causality of the features, a negative control with permuted lives, and exact reproduction of a refitted fold (Supplementary Section S4); the same four were run on every dataset of the comparison with the metric *M* (Supplementary Section S17). For that comparison, thirty random 80/20 splits of FD001 show the spread of an evaluation on 20 test units; because they overlap, they are summarised by their distribution, not by a pooled interval.`));

// ---- text moved to the supplement (Section S19)
function TIMESCALE_SUPP() {
  return [
    `**Fully nested preprocessing (batteries, convention C).** The battery task is defined in physical units (main text Section ⟦S:problem⟧). Within each outer training set, every inner training subset builds its own blocks, its own z-score statistics and its own regressor, with κ computed from that subset only. Its inner validation cells are scored in their own blocks against the same physical window, so the out-of-fold counts of the five inner folds can be summed per setting even when their κ differ. After selection, blocks, statistics and regressor are rebuilt from the complete outer training set, with κ from that set, and the outer test cells are scored once. This is done under cross-validation by cell (five folds, seed 2026) and with each of the three batches held out in turn. Three ways of choosing κ are reported: **rule, nested**, κ by the protocol rule computed from each training subset (inner subsets for selection, the outer training set for the final model); **selected by inner cross-validation**, κ ∈ {2, …, 6} with the largest summed out-of-fold count (ties to the outer rule value, then to the smaller κ); and **fixed**, each κ ∈ {2, …, 6} in turn, as a sensitivity analysis. No failure life outside a training subset enters its time scale or its selection, so neither the outer test estimates nor the inner selections use held-out lives. Alarms can only be raised at block ends, and the remaining life of a block is that at its last cycle; only the smoothing span and the debounce are counted in blocks.`,
    `**Battery cost.** The battery cost analysis (main text Tables ⟦T:cost⟧ and ⟦T:batcost⟧) is fully nested in the same way, with the task, the lead times and every threshold in cycles; an earlier version that took κ from the outer training set and expressed the window-tuned task in blocks (convention B) is kept in Section S11 for provenance. The battery timing controls and shift study (main text Section ⟦S:q2⟧) still take κ from the outer training cells, with the task in blocks (window [−13, +2] blocks, warning levels in blocks).`,
    `**Bearings.** The bearing analyses use κ from the outer training set (inner folds inherit it), and a sensitivity analysis holds the physical task fixed for κ ∈ {3, 5, 7, 10, 14}. Inheriting κ can influence which settings are selected, but not the outer test estimate. We did not repeat the bearing analysis with full nesting, because every variant run gave at most one correct alarm in 17.`,
    `**How an operator would choose κ.** In service, lives are unknown, but three sources of information are not: the lives of units previously run to failure (the training fleet, as here), design or warranty lives, and the notice that a replacement needs. We therefore regard the task as fixed in physical units and κ as a temporal resolution, chosen from historical lives or by cross-validation on them. For the batteries the all-cell median life (${BD.life_median} cycles) lies just below the boundary between κ = 3 and κ = 4 (733.25 cycles), which is why the protocol value and the training-only values differ in some splits.`];
}

