B.push(P(`**Answer to Q1.** On PHM08 the contrast between long-range fixed-time prediction (${f1(FMV.fixed_f50.pct)}% at half of life under cross-fitting; ${f1(halfMax)}% for the best of eleven models under the fit-75%/calibrate-25% protocol, against ${f1(FMV.recipe_self_7525.pct)}% for the recipe under that protocol) and the self-timed alarm (${f1(FMV.recipe_self.pct)}%) is mostly a contrast of observation time, with a further, smaller contribution from target and feature design, which was larger on FD004. A study that compares a fixed-time model scored at a fraction of life with an alarm scored at its own time therefore compares two prediction problems of different difficulty. What self-timing contributes is not accuracy but the ability to act without knowing the life: a fraction of life cannot be observed in service, whereas the alarm reaches near-failure accuracy from the history alone, with no missed engine and a median of ${FMV.recipe_self.median_left} cycles of notice on PHM08. Whether such an alarm is a good maintenance policy is a separate question (Section ⟦S:q3⟧). These are paired comparisons on 218 and 249 engines of one simulator family, and differences of a few points could go undetected.`));
