B.push(P(`**Tuning to the window acts on the timing.** Tuning selects, among other settings, the shift *s* added to the smoothed estimate, and Proposition 1 describes its effect for a fixed error distribution. We varied the shift on the test units of each fold by up to ±4 around the value selected on training data at *H* = 20, keeping the other settings, and recorded the alarm cycle, the true remaining life at the alarm and the unshifted error ε (Fig. ⟦F:shift⟧). On each of the seven datasets the shift acted mainly by moving the alarm. Each unit of shift changed the median true remaining life at the alarm by ${f1(Math.min(...shR))} to ${f1(Math.max(...shR))} cycles (blocks for the battery), against ${f1(Math.min(...shE))} to ${f1(Math.max(...shE))} for the median of ε. Assumption A1 is therefore violated by construction, yet the error at the alarm changed slowly. Within ±2 of the selected shift, the largest difference between the realised rate and the rate predicted by Proposition 1 from the errors at the selected shift was ${f1(Math.min(...shG2))} to ${f1(Math.max(...shG2))} percentage points, depending on the dataset (largest on ${RNAME[shG2max]}); within ±4 it was ${f1(Math.min(...shG))} to ${f1(Math.max(...shG))} points. The proposition thus describes the direction and rough size of the response, with errors of several points; it is not a bound for the sequential policy. In a component ablation on PHM08, removing the shift caused the largest loss among the trigger and target components (Supplementary Section S15).`));
B.push(...withId('shift', figure(__dirname + '/img/Fig8_shift_A1.png', 'What tuning the shift acts on: test units at *H* = 20 with the other settings fixed. (a) Realised in-window rate (solid) and the rate predicted by Proposition 1 from the errors at the selected shift (dashed) as the shift moves away from the value selected on training data. (b) Change of the median true remaining life at the alarm relative to the selected shift; dotted: one cycle less per unit of shift. Battery in blocks of κ from the outer training cells (convention B).')));
B.push(P(`**What the score conceals.** A correct alarm with *H* = 20 means a remaining life of roughly 9 to 31 cycles; the score does not distinguish 10 from 30 cycles of notice, nor say whether the notice suffices for a given lead time (Fig. ⟦F:errlead⟧). It treats every error inside [−13, +2] alike and every error outside it alike, whatever its size, and it is silent about cost. The warning level trades score for notice: on PHM08, lower levels gave higher rates but less notice (${f1(cv.PHM08['10'].pct)}% at *H* = 10), and *H* = 30 and 40 gave ${f1(cv.PHM08['30'].pct)}% and ${f1(cv.PHM08['40'].pct)}%; on FD002 and FD003 the rate did not fall monotonically with *H* (Fig. ⟦F:level⟧). Section ⟦S:battery⟧ shows, on the battery cells, a case in which the policy with the higher window score gave much less notice, and Section ⟦S:q3⟧ shows what the score leaves out about cost.`));
B.push(...withId('level', figure(__dirname + '/img/Fig4_level_tradeoff.png', 'Share of first warnings in the window as a function of the warning level *H* (five-fold cross-validation). A higher level asks for more notice. Error bars: 95% Clopper–Pearson intervals for the pre-specified N-CMAPSS analysis.', 3.4)));
B.push(...withId('errlead', figure(__dirname + '/img/Fig5_error_leadtime.png', 'Distribution of (a) the warning error and (b) the true remaining life at the alarm at *H* = 20 for PHM08 (five-fold cross-validation) and N-CMAPSS (pre-specified analysis). Shaded: the window; dotted: 13 cycles, below which the early limit practically cannot bind. Alarms with the same window outcome can give very different notice.')));
B.push(P(`**Answer to Q2.** For a threshold-crossing alarm, the asymmetric window score is driven mainly by when the alarm fires. The reported estimate added little that a constant could not supply, and tuning to the window acted mainly on the alarm time. The timing itself depended on the regressor, so the score does separate good from poor timing mechanisms; what it does not measure is the accuracy of the remaining-life estimate, the notice given or the cost incurred.`));
