B.push(H1('7. Maintenance-policy value (Q3)', 'q3'));
B.push(P(`A score is a useful surrogate for maintenance only if optimising it produces policies with good decision outcomes. We test this in two ways. Section ⟦S:renewal⟧ prices the alarms in the renewal–reward model of Section ⟦S:cost⟧ and compares window tuning with age replacement and with tuning the same regressor directly to cost. Sections ⟦S:mconv⟧–⟦S:frag⟧ evaluate the alarms under the decision-oriented metric *M* of Kamariotis et al. (2024), with their decision grid, against their probability-threshold policies built on the same regressor.`));
COST_SECTION();
B.push(H2('7.2. The decision-oriented metric *M* and its relation to our cost ratio', 'mconv'));
B.push(P(`Kamariotis et al. (2024) assess a prognostic algorithm through the maintenance decisions that a policy takes with its predictions. Decisions can be taken only at times *t*_{k} = *k*Δ*T*; at each one the policy replaces the component preventively, at cost *c*_{p}, or lets it run; a component that fails first is replaced correctively at cost *c*_{c} > *c*_{p}. With *C*_{i} the cost and *T*_{i} the length of the life cycle of test unit *i*, the long-run cost rate is estimated by *R* = Σ*C*_{i} / Σ*T*_{i}, and the metric is its relative excess over a perfect policy that replaces every unit at the last decision time before its failure,`));
B.push(EQ('*M* = ( *R* − *R*_{perf} ) / *R*_{perf}.'));
B.push(P(`They use *M* to compare algorithms, to choose policy thresholds on training units and to tune hyperparameters, on FD001 split into 80 training and 20 test engines with Δ*T* = 10 cycles, and they state that an evaluation of *M* on 20 units is subject to significant statistical uncertainty. The status of our comparison is as follows. **Re-implemented:** the metric with its first-order variance (their Eqs. 2–9), the grid-perfect reference, and their policies 1 and 3, applied on FD001–FD004 to our regressor under their conventions. **Reproduced:** only the qualitative findings of their simulation study, with our implementation on their simulator (Section ⟦S:mpol⟧). **Assumed:** a construction of the failure probability from our point regressor, the mapping of our alarms to decision times, and a grid of cost ratios (below). **Not verifiable:** their code, data split and prognostic models are not available to us, so we do not compare with the values they report, which come from other models on one 20-engine split, and the published version of their article should be checked against the preprint used here. Everything in this section is exploratory; the analysis plans, including seeds and the definition of a win, were hashed before the runs (Table ⟦T:status⟧).`));
B.push(P(`With ℓ = 0 and ρ = *c*_{c}/*c*_{p}, our cost rate relative to perfect foresight (Section ⟦S:cost⟧) and *M* differ only in the reference policy. Ours replaces at *L* − 1; theirs at the last decision time before *L*. Hence the ratio of Section ⟦S:cost⟧ equals (1 + *M*)(1 + *g*), where *g* = Σ(*L*_{i} − 1) / Σ*T*_{i}^{perf} − 1 is a property of the test lives and the grid alone. The ratio equals 1 + *M* only for Δ*T* = 1. With Δ*T* = 10, *g* was ${f1(Math.min(...geRange))}–${f1(Math.max(...geRange))}% on PHM08 and C-MAPSS and ${f1(GE['NCMAPSS6|10'])}% on N-CMAPSS, whose lives of 48–100 flight cycles make a 10-cycle grid coarse; we therefore report no *M* for N-CMAPSS. Three further differences matter. (1) **Decision grid.** Our alarms can fire at any cycle. On their grid an alarm acts at the next decision time, and an alarm whose decision time is at or beyond the failure counts as a failure. Without this mapping *M* can be negative: in the earlier battery cost analysis (convention B) with Δ*T* = 30 cycles, the unmapped capacity-threshold policy reached *M* = ${pct2(R5.grid_negative.min_pct)}%, because its alarms fell between decision times. Our policies were therefore re-selected on training units under the grid-mapped cost. (2) **Lead time.** Their replacement-only setting has no lead time, so the comparison uses ℓ = 0 only; their ordering setting, which has a different lead-time model, was not implemented. (3) **Cost ratios.** We use *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} ∈ {${QS7.join(', ')}}, chosen to span the ranges shown in their figures; 0.1, the only ratio for which their text reports a value, is primary and the others are descriptive.`));
B.push(H2('7.3. Their policies on our regressor', 'mpol'));
B.push(P(`Their policy 1 replaces at the first decision time at which Pr(RUL ≤ Δ*T*) ≥ *p*_{thres}, with *p*_{thres} = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} or a threshold optimised on training units; their policy 3 replaces when the replacement time that minimises the expected cost under the predictive RUL distribution, with the remaining life valued at an age-replacement cost rate, falls before the next decision time (their Eq. 14, option 1). Both need a failure probability, which our point regressor does not give. We built it in two ways from training units only: **P-cls**, a gradient-boosted classifier of RUL ≤ 10 on the recipe's features, calibrated by isotonic regression on inner out-of-fold predictions; and **P-res**, the empirical distribution of the true RUL in equal-count bins of the inner out-of-fold estimates of the cap-60 regressor (at least 200 rows per bin, monotonicity enforced, RUL above the cap set to the cap; cap 125 as a sensitivity analysis for policy 3). Differences between their policies and ours therefore reflect the probability model as much as the policy. Optimised thresholds were chosen on the inner out-of-fold predictions; when several thresholds tied, the middle of the tied range was used, and the largest tied threshold, which corresponds to the choice shown in their Fig. 9a, was computed as well. A policy is said to beat another only when the paired 95% bootstrap interval of the difference in *M* (2,000 resamples of test units, the grid-perfect reference recomputed in each) excludes zero in the five-fold run, which tests each unit once. On their simulator (their Section 3) our implementation reproduced their qualitative findings at all seven ratios: the optimised policy 1 had the lowest *M* and policy 3 was below policy 2. There, as in their study, the threshold was optimised and scored on the same simulated units, so that agreement partly reflects in-sample optimisation.`));
B.push(H2('7.4. Results under *M*', 'mres'));
