B.push(P(`**FD001.** At *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} = ${Q0} (Table ⟦T:mtab⟧), their policy 1 with P-res reached *M* = ${pct2(r1.M)}% without a failure, below our 1-SE cost-tuned alarm (${pct2(s1.M)}%) and our window-tuned alarm (${pct2(w1.M)}%); in both comparisons the paired interval excluded zero (Table ⟦T:mpairs⟧), so on FD001 **their policy beats ours**. It did so at ${KM.fd001.tally[`STW_window|${P1R}`].theirs} of the seven ratios against the window-tuned alarm and ${KM.fd001.tally[`STW_cost_1se|${P1R}`].theirs} against the 1-SE alarm, and ours never beat it. Our alarms beat their policy 3 (${pct2(p31.M)}%) and age replacement (${pct2(kt('fd001', Q0, 'age_grid').M)}%). Against policy 1 with P-cls (${pct2(c1.M)}%, ${c1.failures} failure) the comparison was unclear at every ratio: one failure among 100 units widens the interval to tens of points. Two observations explain the result. First, the grid penalises an alarm that was not designed for it: our window-tuned alarm, scored at its own cycle, reached ${pct2(wu1.M)}%, but such an alarm is not admissible on their grid, and mapped to it, with settings re-selected for the grid, it reached ${pct2(w1.M)}%. Second, with bins about 10 cycles wide near failure, P-res policy 1 amounts to replacing at the first decision time at which the regressor's estimate falls below about ${f1(KM.fd001.bins.hi[1])} cycles: the same regressor, used through a simple grid-aware rule, gave lower *M* than through our trigger. The interval of their Eq. 7 treats *R*_{perf} as fixed. It was ±${pct2(kt('fd001', Q0, 'perfect_grid').eq7[1])} points even for the perfect policy, whose *M* is 0 by definition, which is why our verdicts use the paired bootstrap.`));
const otherTheirs = verdictDs('STW_window', P1R, 'theirs').filter(d => d !== 'fd001'), otherUnclear = verdictDs('STW_window', P1R, 'unclear'), otherMine = verdictDs('STW_window', P1R, 'mine');
const se_th = verdictDs('STW_cost_1se', P1R, 'theirs'), se_un = verdictDs('STW_cost_1se', P1R, 'unclear'), se_mi = verdictDs('STW_cost_1se', P1R, 'mine');
const p3mine = verdictDs('STW_window', 'P3_res60', 'mine'), clsTheirs = verdictDs('STW_window', P1C, 'theirs'), clsMine = verdictDs('STW_window', P1C, 'mine');
B.push(P(`**FD002–FD004 and the spread over splits.** At the primary ratio, policy 1 with P-res beat the window-tuned alarm on ${listDs(verdictDs('STW_window', P1R, 'theirs'))}${otherUnclear.length ? `, the difference was unclear on ${listDs(otherUnclear)}` : ''}${otherMine.length ? `, and the window-tuned alarm beat it on ${listDs(otherMine)}` : ''}. Against the 1-SE alarm it won on ${listDs(se_th)}${se_un.length ? `, was unclear on ${listDs(se_un)}` : ''}${se_mi.length ? ` and lost on ${listDs(se_mi)}` : ''}. Our window-tuned alarm beat policy 3 on ${listDs(p3mine)} and age replacement on all four datasets. Against policy 1 with P-cls it ${clsMine.length ? `won on ${listDs(clsMine)}` : 'never won'}${clsTheirs.length ? `, lost on ${listDs(clsTheirs)}` : ''} and was otherwise unclear. The lowest *M* at this ratio was reached by ${andList(bestTxt())} (Table ⟦T:mtab⟧), so neither family of policies was best everywhere, and on FD004 no difference between our window-tuned alarm and their policy 1 was clear. On thirty random 80/20 splits of FD001, which overlap and are therefore summarised without a pooled interval (Supplementary Section S17), the median *M* at ${Q0} was ${pct2(rsq(P1C).med)}% for policy 1 with P-cls, ${pct2(rsq(P1R).med)}% with P-res, ${pct2(rsq('STW_cost_1se').med)}% for the 1-SE alarm and ${pct2(rsq('STW_window').med)}% for the window-tuned alarm. A failure among the 20 test engines occurred in ${rsq(P1C).fail_splits}, ${rsq(P1R).fail_splits}, ${rsq('STW_cost_1se').fail_splits} and ${rsq('STW_window').fail_splits} of the 30 splits, respectively, and raised *M* in such a split to as much as ${f1(Math.max(...[P1C, P1R, 'STW_cost_1se', 'STW_window'].map(p => rsq(p).max)))}%, which illustrates the uncertainty of a single 20-unit evaluation that Kamariotis et al. point out.`));
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7.4 robustness of direct threshold optimisation (Phase 3)
const TL = (d, prot, p) => FRG.tally[`${d}|${prot}|${p}`];
