must(CVc.fail_train_next >= 1 && CVc.M_train_next > 10 * CVc.M_train_max, 'their Fig. 9a pattern on our data: one threshold step beyond the optimised plateau makes training failures and a very large M (FD001 fold 1, c_p/c_c = 0.01, P-cls)');
must(tieDiff === 0 || tieDiff > 0, 'tie rules compared on every dataset');
B.push(H2('7.5. Robustness of direct threshold optimisation', 'frag'));
B.push(P(`Their Fig. 9a shows the risk of optimising *p*_{thres} when failures are expensive: the training optimum lies at the edge of a plateau, and a slightly larger threshold lets a unit or two fail and makes *M* very large. The same shape appeared on our data (Fig. ⟦F:mfig⟧b; FD001, fold 1, *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} = 0.01, P-cls, chosen before the run). Every threshold from ${CVc.p_mid < CVc.p_max ? `${CVc.p_mid.toPrecision(2)} to ${CVc.p_max.toPrecision(2)}` : CVc.p_max.toPrecision(2)} gave the minimal training *M* of ${pct2(CVc.M_train_max)}%; the next threshold, ${CVc.next_p.toPrecision(3)}, let ${CVc.fail_train_next} of the ${CVc.n_train} training engines fail and raised the training *M* to ${f1(CVc.M_train_next)}%. With P-res the step was larger (${CVr.fail_train_next} training failures and *M* = ${f1(CVr.M_train_next)}% just above the plateau), because its probabilities take only a few values. The largest tied threshold, the choice their Fig. 9a illustrates, therefore sat one grid step from the training cliff; the middle of the tie sat further from it. On the test engines of that fold neither choice produced a failure.`));
B.push(P(`Across test sets the risk materialised unevenly (Fig. ⟦F:mfig⟧c). At *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} ≤ 0.1, the five folds of FD001–FD004 give 80 fold–ratio cells for each policy. Policy 1 with an optimised threshold had at least one test failure in ${sumT('P1_cls_opt_mid')} of them with P-cls and ${sumT('P1_res_opt_mid')} with P-res, against ${sumT('P1_cls_q')} and ${sumT('P1_res_q')} with *p*_{thres} = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c}; plain cost tuning of our alarm had a failure in ${sumT('STW_cost')}, the one-standard-error rule in ${sumT('STW_cost_1se')} and window tuning in ${sumT('STW_window')}. Over the thirty random FD001 splits the corresponding counts, of 120 split–ratio cells, were ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'P1_cls_opt_mid').fail_small} and ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'P1_res_opt_mid').fail_small} for optimised policy 1, ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'P1_cls_q').fail_small} and ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'P1_res_q').fail_small} with *p*_{thres} = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c}, ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'STW_cost').fail_small} for plain cost tuning, ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'STW_cost_1se').fail_small} with the one-standard-error rule and ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'STW_window').fail_small} for window tuning. In ${M50share}% of the five-fold cells with a failure, that failure raised *M* above 50%. By the decision rule fixed before the run (each plainly optimised policy must fail in more cells than its regularised or heuristic counterpart), the claim that direct cost tuning is fragile was ${supD.length === 4 ? 'supported on all four datasets' : supD.length ? `supported on ${listDs(supD)} and weakened on ${listDs(weakD)}` : 'weakened on all four datasets'}${rsv ? (rsv.startsWith('weakened') && supD.length === 0 ? ' and over the thirty splits' : `, and ${rsv.replace(' (descriptive)', '')} over the thirty splits`) : ''}. ${STWfrag ? 'Plain cost tuning of our alarms was clearly more fragile than the one-standard-error rule' : 'Plain cost tuning of our alarms was not clearly more fragile than the one-standard-error rule'}; for policy 1 the comparison went the other way: with *p*_{thres} = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} it had a test failure in ${sumT('P1_cls_q')} cells with P-cls and ${sumT('P1_res_q')} with P-res, against ${sumT('P1_cls_opt_mid')} and ${sumT('P1_res_opt_mid')} with optimised thresholds. Window-tuned alarms had a failure in ${sumT('STW_window')} of the 80 five-fold cells but in ${TL('fd001', 'rs', 'STW_window').fail_small} of the 120 split cells, so the margin that window tuning builds in did not always hold. Measured in cycles, the plainly tuned threshold lay a median of ${f1(FRG.stw_margin.STW_cost.median)} cycles above the largest threshold at which a test unit failed, against ${f1(FRG.stw_margin.STW_cost_1se.median)} cycles with the one-standard-error rule. The thresholds that minimised the training cost lay below *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} at the larger ratios (median ${KM.fd001.thresholds['0.1|cls'].mid.toPrecision(2)} for P-cls on FD001 at *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} = 0.1), as in their simulations, so the heuristic replaced later there. The fragility therefore concerns plain minimisation of an empirical cost over many settings, which a one-standard-error rule mitigated, more than the optimisation of a single probability threshold. The middle and the largest tied thresholds gave different test decisions in ${tieDiff} of ${tieDec.toLocaleString('en-US')} cases on FD001–FD004 (largest difference in *M* ${f1(Math.max(...KDS.flatMap(d => ['cls', 'res'].map(m => FRG.tie[`${d}|cv|${m}`].max_abs_M_diff)))) } points)${rsTie ? ` and in ${rsTie.d === 0 ? 'none' : rsTie.d} of ${rsTie.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} over the thirty FD001 splits` : ''}, so the tie rule did not drive these results.`));
B.push(...withId('mfig', figure(__dirname + '/img/Fig10_metric_M.png', `Comparison with the metric *M* of Kamariotis et al. (Δ*T* = 10 cycles, no lead time). (a) *M* at *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} = 0.1 on FD001–FD004 under five-fold cross-validation, with bootstrap 95% intervals, for their policies 1 and 3 on our regressor and for our alarms mapped to the decision grid. (b) Their Fig. 9a on our data: *M* of policy 1 (P-cls) as a function of *p*_{thres} on the training engines (solid) and the test engines (dashed) of FD001 fold 1 at *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} = 0.01; vertical lines: the middle and the largest of the tied optimal thresholds. (c) Number of the 20 fold–ratio cells (five folds, *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} ≤ 0.1) with at least one test failure, by policy and dataset.`)));
B.push(P(`**Answer to Q3.** Optimising the window score produced useful maintenance policies where the alarms were accurate and the test units resembled the training units: window-tuned alarms were cheaper than optimal age replacement in most cost settings on six of seven datasets, but not on N-CMAPSS. It was not equivalent to optimising cost. Tuning the same regressor directly to cost was cheaper on some datasets and more expensive on others; under *M*, a simple grid-aware probability-threshold policy built on the same regressor had a lower *M* on ${listDs(winTheirs)} and a higher *M* on ${listDs(winMine)}; and no tuning rule or policy family was best on every dataset. The window score is therefore an operational specification of when alarms should fire, not a measure of decision value.`));
