B.push(P(`**Under the cost model** (Table ⟦T:batcost⟧; Supplementary Section S11 plots the cost against the lead time), with fully nested preprocessing and every threshold and lead time in cycles, the trend was again the cheaper signal. Under cross-validation each of the four trend policies had a lower point estimate than its recipe counterpart in all 16 lead-time and cost-ratio cells; the interval excluded equality in ${BSc4[3]} of 16 cells for the window-tuned pair, ${BSc4[0]} for the cost-tuned trend with the one-standard-error rule against the window-tuned recipe, ${BSc4[1]} against the recipe with the same rule, and ${BSc4[2]} for plain cost tuning of both. With a batch held out the corresponding counts were ${BSl4[3]}, ${BSl4[0]}, ${BSl4[1]} and ${BSl4[2]} of 16, and no trend policy was significantly dearer than its recipe counterpart in any cell. The fully nested window-tuned recipe was less fragile on a held-out batch than in the earlier analysis with κ from the outer training cells: it let ${bc('lobo', 0, 10, 'STW_window').failures} of 123 cells fail without lead time at ρ = 10 and ${bc('lobo', 30, 50, 'STW_window').failures} with a lead time of 30 cycles at ρ = 50 (earlier ${BC2.lobo.cells['0|10'].STW_window.failures} and ${BC2.lobo.cells['30|50'].STW_window.failures}), and with a lead time of ${[...new Set(worseCells.map(k => k.split('|')[0]))].join(', ')} cycles its point estimate was below that of the window-tuned trend at ${worseCells.length === 4 ? 'every cost ratio' : `${worseCells.length} of the four cost ratios`}, without a significant difference. Plain cost tuning of the recipe let ${bc('lobo', 0, 10, 'STW_cost').failures} cells fail at ℓ = 0, ρ = 10, and ${bc('lobo', 30, 50, 'STW_cost').failures} at ℓ = 30, ρ = 50, and the capacity threshold with the one-standard-error rule let ${bc('lobo', 30, 50, 'capacity_cost_1se').failures} fail at ℓ = 30, ρ = 50. Age replacement, with its replacement age chosen on the other batches, let ${bc('lobo', 0, 10, 'age').failures} cells fail with a batch held out. On these cells, then, a physically motivated baseline aimed at the failure definition did better than the learned recipe on window accuracy across batches and, in most cost settings, on cost, whereas the recipe gave more notice within batches.`));
