B.push(P(`**Bearings.** The recipe failed. None of the 17 first warnings fell in the window, either in the protocol analysis or with κ from the training bearings (${FGc.early} too early and ${FGc.missed} missed). The competitors were also almost always wrong (age-only ${GS['cv|age_only|20'].IN}, MLP with Kalman filter ${GS['cv|mlp_kf|20'].IN} of 17). With the physical task fixed (*H* = 140 snapshots), block lengths of ${Object.keys(GSn).sort((a, b) => a - b).join(', ')} snapshots gave at most ${Math.max(...Object.values(GSn).map(o => o.IN))} of 17. The pre-specified fifth hypothesis, that STW-T would be no more than 5 points worse, was met only trivially (${T1.stwt}% against ${T1.stw}%), which cannot establish non-inferiority. A post hoc diagnosis with an ad hoc criterion (Supplementary Section S7) found that in ${shortOn.length} of 17 bearings the vibration level rose to and stayed above 1.5 times its early-life level only within the last six blocks before failure (about seven minutes), or never. ${sMiss.length} of these were never warned, which is consistent with failure becoming visible too late for a 20-block alarm. The other ${sEarly.length} received alarms ${Math.round(Math.min(...sEarly.map(r => r.left)))}–${Math.round(Math.max(...sEarly.map(r => r.left)))} blocks before failure, long before any sustained rise, which late visibility cannot explain. Of the ${longOn.length} bearings with longer degradation, ${lMiss.length} were missed and ${lEarly.length} warned too early. Late onset is therefore at most a partial explanation; small training sets (13–14 bearings per fold), three operating conditions and the feature set are competing explanations that were not tested. The result shows that this recipe, with these features, did not work on these bearings, not that advance warning of bearing failure is impossible.`));
