B.push(H2('9.1. What the study establishes', 'establish'));
B.push(P(`On the same alarms, the five properties of Section ⟦S:dims⟧ gave different answers. Long-range accuracy was low and near-failure accuracy high for the same model, so a comparison across observation times measured mainly the difficulty of the prediction problem. The window score of a threshold-crossing alarm was driven by its timing, not by the estimate it reported. Maintenance value depended on the cost model, the decision grid and the dataset, and it was not ranked reliably by the window score. And the accuracy, notice and cost of the alarms changed when the test population changed. The practical consequence is that a single number, whether a window score, a fixed-time error or a cost ratio, cannot certify an end-of-life alarm. The evidence is limited to simulated and laboratory run-to-failure data, one family of threshold-crossing alarms and one acceptance window (Section ⟦S:limits⟧), and several results differed between datasets, which is itself part of the finding.`));

B.push(H2('9.2. Why alarm-window scores can mislead', 'mislead'));
B.push(P(`Part of the result is built into the score. For an alarm that fires when a smoothed estimate first crosses a fixed level, the reported estimate at the alarm is close to that level by construction, so the window condition becomes, approximately, a condition on the true remaining life at the alarm. What is not built in is how far this goes and what follows from it. The constant-estimate control measured it: over seven datasets the model's estimate added at most ${f1(Math.max(...constGap.map(Math.abs)))} percentage points, none significant. Tuning to the window then acts mainly on the alarm time, as the shift study showed. Neither of these makes the score uninformative. On PHM08 and FD001–FD004 it separated a regressor with good timing from linear health indicators with poor timing, even when those were calibrated to cycles and given the same trigger, so it is a meaningful measure of timing at the requested level. The misreading arises when the score is taken as evidence of an accurate remaining-life trajectory, of adequate notice or of low maintenance cost, none of which it measures.`));
B.push(P(`The observation-time result shows a second way in which a comparison can mislead. A fixed-time model scored at half of life and an alarm scored at its own time are evaluated on prediction problems of different difficulty: on PHM08 the same calibrated model went from ${f1(FMV.fixed_f50.pct)}% at half of life to ${f1(FMV.fixed_f90.pct)}% at 90% of life${f4rep1 && f4rep2 ? `, and on FD004 from ${f1(F4.fixed_f50.pct)}% to ${f1(F4.fixed_f90.pct)}%` : ''}. A large apparent advantage of an alarm over a fixed-time model can therefore come from the time at which accuracy is measured rather than from the alarm. Under matched training and calibration, self-timing added no measurable accuracy over the same regressor at 90% of life${f4rep2 ? ' on either dataset' : ' on PHM08'}${F4p['recipe_self|recipe_r20'].p < 0.05 ? ', and on FD004 it fell short of the regressor at the oracle time of 20 cycles left' : ''}. Its value is operational: it reaches near-failure accuracy without knowing the life.`));

B.push(H2('9.3. Implications for maintenance decisions', 'maint'));
B.push(P(`Kamariotis et al. (2024) argue that prognostic algorithms and the policies built on them should be judged and tuned by the long-run cost of the decisions they induce. Our results support that view and add three qualifications. First, how the cost is minimised matters. Minimising the empirical cost rate over about two thousand settings sometimes selected alarms with too little margin and produced large losses when failures were expensive; a one-standard-error rule reduced the largest losses, and window tuning was cheaper than that rule in most cells only on ${dsList(ws1se)}. For their policy 1, by contrast, the optimised probability threshold failed on test units less often than the heuristic one${must(sumT('P1_cls_q') > sumT('P1_cls_opt_mid') && sumT('P1_res_q') > sumT('P1_res_opt_mid'), 'policy 1: heuristic threshold had more failing cells than the optimised one, both probability models') || ''}, so the fragility concerns plain minimisation over many settings rather than threshold optimisation as such. Second, the decision rule matters. With the same regressor, a threshold on the probability of failure before the next decision time gave a lower *M* than our self-timed trigger on ${listDs(thW5)}${miW5.length ? ` and a higher *M* on ${listDs(miW5)}` : ''}. Where our trigger lost, it had been chosen for accuracy on the window, not for the notice that the decision grid requires, and it fires between decision times: on FD001, mapping it to the grid raised its *M* from ${pct2(wu1d.M)}% to ${pct2(wm1d.M)}%. Where it won, the narrow late side of the window plausibly built in a safety margin, but that margin did not always hold (Section ⟦S:frag⟧). Third, the signal can matter more than the tuning criterion: on the battery cells, window-tuned and cost-tuned versions of a capacity trend were cheaper than the corresponding recipe policies in every cost setting under cross-validation and never significantly more expensive with a batch held out.`));
B.push(P(`For reliability engineering practice this means that an alarm score should not be used as a stand-in for decision cost. Where cost ratios, lead times and decision times can be stated, the alarm should be evaluated, and if possible tuned, under them, with a safeguard against selection noise, and compared with age replacement and with a policy that uses the decision times explicitly. Where they cannot be stated, a window remains a reasonable operational specification of when alarms should fire, because its settings other than the warning level do not depend on uncertain cost ratios, but its score should then be reported as a timing result. When a decision-oriented metric such as *M* is reported, the decision grid, the perfect reference and the mapping of alarms to decision times should be stated, because they change its value by several percentage points (Section ⟦S:mconv⟧).`));

B.push(H2('9.4. Transfer and generalisation', 'transfer'));
B.push(P(`The transfer results are the most consequential for practice, because a validation within familiar conditions is what an operator usually has. On the battery cells, the held-out-batch rate of the recipe was ${Math.round(Math.min(...gapK))} to ${Math.round(Math.max(...gapK))} percentage points below the within-batch rate, depending on the time-scale convention, and the held-out estimate itself depended on the convention (${f1(Math.min(...kapVals('lobo')))}–${f1(Math.max(...kapVals('lobo')))}% across fixed κ). The batches differ in charging protocols, rest periods and typical life, which is the kind of difference a new test campaign or production lot brings. A capacity-trend baseline aimed at the failure definition lost little across batches (${f1(BTc.pct)}% to ${f1(BTl.pct)}%), so the loss was specific to the learned recipe, not inherent to the task. On N-CMAPSS, the same simulator family under different operating profiles, the recipe reached ${f1(NW.pct)}%, and on the bearings, a different asset type, it failed. The bearing result is informative as a negative result: an alarm recipe that works on one asset class gives no assurance for another, and a late onset of visible degradation explained only part of the misses and early alarms (Section ⟦S:ncmb⟧). Within-population cross-validation should therefore be read as an upper reference, and claims of transferability need whole batches, campaigns or asset types held out, with a simple physically motivated baseline where one exists.`));

B.push(H2('9.5. Recommended reporting', 'report'));
B.push(P(`From these results we derive a recommendation for what studies of end-of-life alarms should report (Table ⟦T:report⟧). It is not an established standard and is not complete; it lists the properties that, in this study, could not be inferred from one another, together with the controls that separated them.`));
B.push(...withId('report', table('Recommended reporting for evaluations of end-of-life alarms, derived from this study. Each row is a property that could not be inferred from the others here.',
  ['Property', 'What to report', 'Why: evidence from this study'],
  [['A. Long-range prediction', 'Accuracy at fixed fractions of life or remaining lives, chosen independently of the policy and stated', `The same calibrated model placed ${f1(FMV.fixed_f50.pct)}% of PHM08 estimates in the window at half of life and ${f1(FMV.fixed_f90.pct)}% at 90% of life (Table ⟦T:matched⟧)`],
   ['B. Near-failure estimation', 'Accuracy near failure under the same training data and calibration as the alarm, so that alarm and fixed-time results are comparable', `The self-timed alarm (${f1(FMV.recipe_self.pct)}%) was no more accurate than its regressor at 90% of life (${f1(FMV.recipe_f90.pct)}%) (Table ⟦T:matched⟧)`],
   ['C. Alarm timing and notice', 'Distribution of the true remaining life at the alarm; share of correct alarms with the notice the task needs; missed units', `A correct alarm at *H* = 20 allowed roughly 9–31 cycles of notice; on the battery cells the policy with the higher window score gave less notice (Sections ⟦S:q2⟧, ⟦S:battery⟧)`],
   ['C. Estimate versus timing', 'A control that keeps the alarm times and replaces the reported estimate by a constant', `The constant scored within ${f1(Math.max(...constGap.map(Math.abs)))} points of the model's estimate on seven datasets (Table ⟦T:controls⟧)`],
   ['D. Maintenance-policy value', 'Cost under stated lead times, cost ratios and decision times, against age replacement and a policy tuned directly to cost; for *M*, the decision grid, the perfect reference and the mapping of alarms to decision times', `Rankings changed with the dataset and the probability model; mapping to the grid raised *M* of the window-tuned alarm on FD001 from ${pct2(wu1d.M)}% to ${pct2(wm1d.M)}% (Tables ⟦T:cost⟧, ⟦T:mtab⟧)`],
   ['E. Transfer', 'Results with whole batches, campaigns, operating conditions or asset types held out, against a simple domain baseline', `Battery: ${f1(BRc.pct)}% within batches, ${f1(BRl.pct)}% with a batch held out (${f1(Math.min(...kapVals('lobo')))}–${f1(Math.max(...kapVals('lobo')))}% across fixed κ; ${f1(BSl.pct)}% with κ by inner CV; ${f1(BLo.pct)}% and ${f1(BLc.pct)}% under conventions A and B); the capacity trend did better; bearings 0 of 17 (Tables ⟦T:battery⟧, ⟦T:ncmb⟧)`],
   ['All', 'Selection on training units only, leakage checks, and the status of each analysis (pre-specified, corrected or exploratory), with intervals and what they cover', 'Several conclusions of this study changed when analyses were corrected or extended (Table ⟦T:status⟧; Supplementary Section S16)']],
  [1700, 3400, 3926])));

B.push(H2('9.6. Limitations', 'limits'));
B.push(...LI([
  `• **Scope of the claim.** The evidence concerns one asymmetric window, threshold-crossing alarms built on one gradient-boosted regressor and its variants, and the policies compared here. Alarms of other kinds, such as detectors or policies acting on full predictive distributions, may relate to the window differently, and the five properties may be closer together for other models or data.`,
  `• **No field data.** Six of the eight datasets are simulations from one simulator family (C-MAPSS), and the battery and bearing data are laboratory measurements; no alarm was deployed. On the batteries, end of life is a threshold on a measured quantity. First-warning rates of the recipe ranged from 0% to ${Math.round(Math.max(BRc.pct, BSc.pct, BWo.pct, ...DS.map(d => cv[d]['20'].pct)))}% across datasets and analyses and fell under a change of test campaign, so they must be re-estimated for each application.`,
  '• **Development, pre-specification and corrections.** The recipe and grids were developed on PHM08, and the primary warning level was designated after the C-MAPSS results were known. Only three datasets had protocols fixed in advance; the hashes were not deposited with a registry, so their timing rests on the authors\' records, and they do not constitute a formal preregistration. The training-only κ and the battery analyses with a common physical task and fully nested preprocessing are corrections made after the protocol analyses. The matched decompositions, the timing controls, the capacity baselines, the cost analyses, the shift study and the comparison with *M* are exploratory; for the most recent of them the plan was hashed before the run. The bearing analysis and the battery timing controls and shift study are not fully nested (Section ⟦S:timescale⟧).',
  '• **Comparison with M.** It is a re-implementation without the reference code, splits or prognostic models; their published version should be checked against the preprint used here for any change to the policy definitions. Their policies need a failure probability, which we built from our regressor in two ways, and the verdicts differed between the two. Our alarms fire between decision times and had to be mapped to a grid of 10 cycles, which their policies use by construction; the grid also changes the perfect reference, by about 2% on the C-MAPSS datasets and by more than 7% on N-CMAPSS, which we therefore excluded. Only the replacement-only setting without lead time was examined, the cost ratios were chosen to span the ranges shown in their figures, and their caution about evaluations on 20 test units applies to our thirty random splits and to any comparison with their reported values. FD002–FD004 were evaluated by five-fold cross-validation only.',
  '• **Scope of the decomposition.** The matched comparison of Q1 uses two datasets of one simulator family (PHM08 and FD004), one fixed-time model and one recipe. Differences of a few points could go undetected with about 220–250 engines.',
  '• **Competitors and baselines.** The challenge approaches are re-implementations, not the original code, and only the networks\' caps and warning settings were tuned. The health-indicator, calibrated-indicator, capacity-threshold and capacity-trend baselines are single representatives of their classes, and the capacity trend exists only for the batteries.',
  '• **Statistics.** Intervals are conditional on the fitted policies; refitting and fold-assignment variability is reflected only in the repeated PHM08 and FD001 splits and the κ sensitivity. Non-significant differences are not evidence of equivalence. Multiplicity is adjusted within tables, not across the paper, and the cost cells or cost ratios within a dataset are correlated, so they are not replications. The interval of their Eq. 7 ignores the variability of the perfect reference and was not used for our verdicts.',
  '• **Cost model.** The renewal–reward model assumes a fixed lead time, independent units and replacement as good as new. The cost ratios are illustrative, and the model ignores spare parts, downtime and the grouping of maintenance across units (de Pater et al., 2022; Mitici et al., 2023). The window-tuned and cost-tuned policies select on the same out-of-fold predictions that tune their other settings.',
  `• **Theory.** Proposition 1 holds for a fixed error distribution. For the sequential policy it is only an approximate description on these data, with errors of up to ${f1(Math.max(...shG))} points within ±4 of the selected shift, not a guarantee.`]));

B.push(H2('9.7. Open questions', 'open'));
B.push(P(`Five questions remain open. **Decision-aware alarms:** a trigger could choose its warning level, or its threshold on a failure probability, for the decision times and lead time of the maintenance task, and be compared with the policies of Kamariotis et al. (2024) in their ordering setting with lead time. **Field data and changing conditions:** whether recalibrating the shift on the first failures of a new population, or attaching distribution-free uncertainty to the alarm time, preserves accuracy and cost needs fleet data with censoring. **Maintenance policies:** the alarms of several units could be grouped into shared visits (de Pater et al., 2022; Mitici et al., 2023), with the comparisons of window, cost and simple baselines repeated under such policies. **Test and inspection policies:** for units that are proof-tested or inspected at intervals (de Jonge & Scarf, 2020), an alarm could trigger an additional test rather than a replacement, which requires a cost model with test costs. **Fault detection:** for failures that become visible only shortly before they happen, as on several bearings, the first-warning rule should be compared with sequential detectors such as cumulative-sum and exponentially weighted moving-average charts (Page, 1954; Roberts, 1959) under the same leakage-safe protocol.`));

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10 Conclusion
B.push(H1('10. Conclusion', 'concl'));
B.push(P(`This study asked what an end-of-life alarm score measures and whether optimising it yields good maintenance decisions. On eight run-to-failure datasets, with every setting selected on training units only, long-range prediction, near-failure estimation, alarm timing, maintenance-policy value and transfer behaved as distinct properties. Observation time explained most of the contrast between fixed-time and alarm evaluation (on PHM08, ${f1(FMV.fixed_f50.pct)}% at half of life against ${f1(FMV.fixed_f90.pct)}% at 90% of life for the same calibrated model), and self-timing added no measurable accuracy. The asymmetric window score was driven by alarm timing: a constant estimate at the same alarm times scored within ${f1(Math.max(...constGap.map(Math.abs)))} percentage points of the model's estimate, and the score revealed neither the notice given nor the cost incurred.`));
B.push(P(`Optimising the window was not equivalent to optimising cost. Window-tuned alarms were cheaper than age replacement in most cost settings on six of seven datasets, but direct cost tuning was better on some datasets and worse on others, and under the re-implemented metric of Kamariotis et al. their probability-threshold policy on our regressor had a lower *M* than our window-tuned alarm on ${listDs(thW5)} (${pct2(kt('fd001', 0.1, 'P1_res_opt_mid').M)}% against ${pct2(kt('fd001', 0.1, 'STW_window').M)}% on FD001, the dataset of their study) and a higher *M* on ${listDs(miW5)}; no policy was best everywhere. Performance validated within familiar conditions did not transfer: on battery cells with fully nested preprocessing the recipe's in-window rate fell from ${f1(BRc.pct)}% to ${f1(BRl.pct)}% with a batch held out (${f1(Math.min(...kapVals('lobo')))}–${f1(Math.max(...kapVals('lobo')))}% across fixed time scales; ${f1(BSl.pct)}%, ${f1(BLo.pct)}% and ${f1(BLc.pct)}% under the other conventions), a capacity-trend baseline did better, and the recipe failed on bearings (0 of 17).`));
B.push(P(`An end-of-life alarm should therefore not be judged by a single score. Studies should report its accuracy far from and near failure, the notice its alarms give, a control that separates the estimate from the timing, its cost under stated decision conditions against age replacement and a cost-tuned policy, and its performance on held-out batches, campaigns or asset types against a simple domain baseline. What remains unresolved is how these properties behave on field data with censoring and changing conditions, and whether alarms designed for the decision times and lead times of the maintenance task close the gap between timing and cost. These conclusions rest on simulations and laboratory tests and support a way of evaluating alarms, not the deployment of the recipe.`));

