// ---------------------------------------------------------------- S16 correction note and change log
function CORRECTION_NOTE() {
  return `**Correction note.** In the protocol analyses of the battery and bearing data, the block length κ was computed from the median life of all units, including the test units of each split, so that test-unit lives influenced the time scale of the task. An intermediate correction computed κ from the outer training units of each split and let the inner folds inherit it, but kept the warning level and the window in blocks, so that the physical task (in cycles or snapshots) changed with κ. For the battery cells, the analysis reported in the main text fixes the task in cycles (*H* = 60, window [−39, +6], lead times 0–45 cycles), computes κ separately in every inner training subset and in the outer training set, and reports κ chosen by inner cross-validation and fixed κ = 2–6 as sensitivity analyses. For the bearings the intermediate correction is retained (Section ⟦S:timescale⟧ of the main text). The table below gives the results under each convention. The protocol and intermediate results are reported for transparency; the fully nested results are the ones interpreted. Two statements in an earlier version of the manuscript are withdrawn because the shift study does not support them: that no choice of shift could rescue a low in-window rate, and that the concentration of the out-of-fold error could serve as a check before deployment. Any reading of the contrast between fixed-time prediction at half of life and the self-timed alarm as an effect of self-timing is not supported by the matched comparison (Section ⟦S:q1⟧ of the main text).`;
}
function CORRECTION_ROWS() {
  const c = o => o ? `${o.IN}/${o.n} (${f1(o.pct)}%)` : '–';
  return [
    ['Battery, recipe, cross-validation by cell', c(BWo), c(BWc), `${c(BRc)}; κ by inner CV ${f1(BSc.pct)}%; fixed κ ${f1(Math.min(...kapVals('cv123')))}–${f1(Math.max(...kapVals('cv123')))}%`],
    ['Battery, recipe, batch held out', c(BLo), c(BLc), `${c(BRl)}; κ by inner CV ${f1(BSl.pct)}%; fixed κ ${f1(Math.min(...kapVals('lobo')))}–${f1(Math.max(...kapVals('lobo')))}%`],
    ['Battery, capacity trend, cross-validation', '– (not in the protocol)', c(BTrB), c(BTc)],
    ['Battery, capacity trend, batch held out', '– (not in the protocol)', c(BTrLB), c(BTl)],
    ['Battery, MLP + Kalman filter, cross-validation', c(bdo['cv123|mlp_kf|20']), c(bw('cv123', 'mlp_kf')), '– (not rerun)'],
    ['Battery, MLP + Kalman filter, batch held out', c(bdo['lobo|mlp_kf|20']), c(bw('lobo', 'mlp_kf')), '– (not rerun)'],
    ['Bearings, recipe, cross-validation', c(gbo['cv|warning|20']), c(FGc), '– (not run; main text Section ⟦S:timescale⟧)'],
    ['Bearings, recipe, official split', c(gbo['official|warning|20']), c(GS['official|warning|20']), '– (not run)']];
}
function CHANGE_LOG() {
  return [
    '• **Time scale.** κ from training units only; battery task fixed in physical units with fully nested preprocessing (main text Sections ⟦S:units⟧, ⟦S:timescale⟧ and ⟦S:battery⟧; Tables S2–S4); results under all conventions above.',
    '• **Fixed-time versus self-timed.** The comparison of fixed-time models fitted on 75% of the training engines with a recipe fitted on all of them was replaced by a matched, cross-fitted comparison that varies observation time, regressor, calibration and trigger one at a time (main text Table ⟦T:matched⟧, Fig. ⟦F:matched⟧); the matched 75%/25% comparison and the full fit-75%/calibrate-25% reference are kept (Table ⟦T:matched⟧, Section S1).',
    '• **Battery baseline.** The capacity trend is evaluated on accuracy and notice under the same physical task and fully nested selection as the recipe (main text Table ⟦T:battery⟧, Fig. ⟦F:batfig⟧), and on cost under the same cost model, lead times, cost ratios, splits and selection rules as the recipe (main text Table ⟦T:batcost⟧; Section S11), with settings chosen on training cells.',
    '• **Shift and Proposition 1.** A comparison of the realised in-window rate with the concentration Pr(|ε − *m*| ≤ 7.5) computed from the same test alarms was removed, because both come from the same alarms and their agreement does not test assumption A1; the shift study (main text Section ⟦S:q2⟧, Fig. ⟦F:shift⟧) shows that the shift moves the alarm, and Proposition 1 is stated as a conditional result.',
    '• **Cost.** Policies tuned to cost (plain, one-standard-error rule, quantile-based) are compared with window-tuned alarms per dataset under identical information (main text Section ⟦S:renewal⟧, Table ⟦T:cost⟧; Section S11).',
    '• **Status of analyses.** Every analysis is labelled as development, pre-specified, exploratory or corrected (main text Table ⟦T:status⟧); the identical-protocol comparison moved to Section S15.',
    '• **This version (reframing).** The title, abstract, introduction, discussion and conclusion were rewritten around the question of what an alarm score measures; the results are organised by question (main text Sections ⟦S:res1⟧–⟦S:q4⟧), the comparison with *M* is part of the answer to Q3, and a recommended reporting table was added (main text Table ⟦T:report⟧). The identical-protocol comparison and ablation (Section S15), the battery cost figure (Section S11) and the details of the battery time-scale conventions (Section S19) moved to this supplement. No analysis was added or rerun, and every number is unchanged.',
    '• **Third revision.** A key-numbers table (main text Table ⟦T:key⟧); a re-implementation of the metric *M* of Kamariotis et al. with their decision grid, their policies 1 and 3 on our regressor, and the comparison on FD001–FD004 (main text Sections ⟦S:mconv⟧–⟦S:frag⟧, Tables ⟦T:mtab⟧–⟦T:mpairs⟧, Fig. ⟦F:mfig⟧; Section S17); the calibrated health-indicator alarm (main text Table ⟦T:controls⟧; Section S18); the battery cost analysis repeated with fully nested preprocessing and the task in cycles, which replaces the earlier version in the main text (main text Table ⟦T:batcost⟧; Section S11); the matched decomposition repeated on FD004 (main text Table ⟦T:matched⟧). Each of these analyses was fixed in a plan hashed before it was run. The sentence stating that our policies were not compared with the framework of Kamariotis et al. was removed, and every battery number is labelled with its time-scale convention.'];
}
