// ---------------------------------------------------------------- S17 comparison with the metric M (Phases 1-3 of the third revision)
S.push(H1('S17. Comparison with the metric M: implementation, checks and all cost ratios'));
const PN7 = { P1_res_opt_mid: 'P1 P-res opt (mid)', P1_res_opt_max: 'P1 P-res opt (max)', P1_res_q: 'P1 P-res *p* = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c}', P1_cls_opt_mid: 'P1 P-cls opt (mid)', P1_cls_opt_max: 'P1 P-cls opt (max)', P1_cls_q: 'P1 P-cls *p* = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c}',
  P3_res60: 'P3 P-res cap 60', P3_res125: 'P3 P-res cap 125', STW_window: 'STW window (mapped)', STW_cost_1se: 'STW cost 1-SE (mapped)', STW_cost: 'STW cost (mapped)', HI_cost: 'HI cost (mapped)', age_grid: 'Age (grid)',
  STW_window_unmapped: 'STW window, unmapped', STW_cost_1se_unmapped: 'STW cost 1-SE, unmapped', STW_cost_unmapped: 'STW cost, unmapped', run_to_failure: 'Run to failure', perfect_grid: 'Grid-perfect' };
const simT = R5.sim_table, simP = ['P1, p_thres = c_p/c_c', 'P1, optimised p_thres', 'P2 (Eq. 12)', 'P3 (Eq. 14, option 1)'];
S.push(P0(`**Implementation.** The metric was re-implemented from the preprint of Kamariotis et al. (arXiv:2306.03759v2): the cost-rate estimator and its first-order variance (their Eqs. 2–3), the perfect policy at the last decision time before failure (Eq. 4), *M* and its variance (Eqs. 6–7, 9), the heuristic policy 1 (Eq. 10), policy 2 (Eq. 12) and policy 3 with option 1 for the cost rate (Eq. 14). Fifteen unit tests pass, including *M* = 0 for the perfect policy, *M* ≥ 0 for 2,000 random grid policies, a hand-computed three-unit example, the recording (not clipping) of negative *M* for an off-grid policy, and the case of their footnote 1 in which letting a unit fail is optimal when *c*_{c} is close to *c*_{p}. That case did not occur in any simulator setting or dataset–ratio combination. **Simulator check.** Their virtual RUL simulator (time to failure normal with mean 225 and standard deviation 40, lognormal multiplicative errors with σ = 0.4 or 0.15 and correlation length 50, Δ*T* = 10, 2,000 components) gave the qualitative pattern of their Figs. 4–5 (next table): the optimised policy 1 had the lowest *M* at all seven ratios for both σ, policy 3 was below policy 2, and the optimised threshold lay below *c*_{p}/*c*_{c} at ${R5.sim.pstar_lt.map(x => `${x[1]} of 7 ratios for σ = ${x[0]}`).join(' and ')}. As in their study, the threshold was optimised and scored on the same simulated components.`));
const simN = { 'P1, p_thres = c_p/c_c': 'Policy 1, *p*_{thres} = *c*_{p}/*c*_{c}', 'P1, optimised p_thres': 'Policy 1, optimised *p*_{thres}', 'P2 (Eq. 12)': 'Policy 2 (Eq. 12)', 'P3 (Eq. 14, option 1)': 'Policy 3 (Eq. 14, option 1)' };
S.push(...tableS('Simulator check (σ = 0.4): *M* (%) of the four policies at each cost ratio, with 95% intervals from their Eq. 7 and the number of the 2,000 components that failed.', ['*c*_{p}/*c*_{c}', ...simP.map(p => simN[p])],
  QS7.map(q => [q, ...simP.map(p => { const o = simT[`0.4|${q}|${p}`]; return `${pct2(o.M)} (${pct2(o.lo)}–${pct2(o.hi)}); ${o.failures}`; })]), [900, 2050, 2050, 2050, 2050]));
S.push(P0(`**Reference-grid effect.** With decisions every Δ*T* = 10 cycles, the grid-perfect policy replaces each unit on average ${['PHM08', 'FD001', 'FD002', 'FD003', 'FD004', 'NCMAPSS6'].map(d => `${f1(R5.grid_effect[`${d}|10`])}% (${RNAME[d]})`).join(', ')} earlier, in total cycles of use, than replacement one cycle before failure; for the battery cells the effect was ${f1(R5.grid_effect['Battery|10'])}% with Δ*T* = 10 cycles and ${f1(R5.grid_effect['Battery|30'])}% with Δ*T* = 30 cycles. For the cost results of main text Section ⟦S:renewal⟧ at ℓ = 0 (unmapped), the ratio relative to the one-cycle reference exceeded 1 + *M* by ${R5.grid_diff.cmapss.map(x => x.toFixed(3)).join('–')} on PHM08 and C-MAPSS and ${R5.grid_diff.ncmapss.map(x => x.toFixed(3)).join('–')} on N-CMAPSS. ${R5.grid_negative.n} unmapped cases had *M* < 0 (the cost-tuned capacity threshold on the battery (convention B) with Δ*T* = 30 cycles at ρ = ${R5.grid_negative.cases.map(c => c.rho).join(', ')}, each ${pct2(R5.grid_negative.min_pct)}%), which is why our alarms are mapped to the grid.`));
S.push(P0(`**Checks of the runs.** In every split the outer test units, the training units and the inner folds were disjoint (E1). On each of FD001–FD004 the features and both probability models were causal (E2), a classifier trained on lives permuted across training engines gave a much larger *M* (E3), and a refit of fold 1 reproduced every decision of every policy at every ratio (E4): ${KDS.map(d => `${d.toUpperCase()}: ${KM[d].leakage.map(l => l.slice(0, 4)).join('/')}`).join('; ')}. No grid-mapped policy had *M* < 0 in any split. After a reset of the computing environment the FD001 runs were repeated; the repetition reproduced the earlier tables exactly (${R5.r0.filter(l => l.startsWith('PASS')).length} files compared, largest difference 0).`));
